The Go code is a rewrite, not a transliteration, but it reproduces libxaac's
algorithms and constants (including its handling of corner cases) and was
verified stage by stage against an instrumented build of libxaac. The constant
tables derived from libxaac are generated from its source text by the scripts
in tools/usacgen/ (gen_esbr_tables.py, gen_mps_tables.py,
gen_usac_tables_xaac.py); every generated file (esbr_tables_gen.go,
mps_tables_gen.go, usac_tables_xaac_gen.go) carries a provenance header and is
kept separate from the hand written code so that it can be replaced. libxaac
also generated the test streams (testdata/usac/xaac_*.m4a, encoded with
libxaac's xaacenc) and the reference output (the .s16 files, decoded with
libxaac's xaacdec) of the tests in usac_xaac_test.go.

Opus decoding
-------------
Opus decoding uses github.com/thesyncim/gopus (BSD-3-Clause).

AV1 decoding
------------
internal/media/av1 is a pure-Go implementation of the AV1 decoding process
written from the AV1 Bitstream & Decoding Process Specification (Alliance for
Open Media, https://aomedia.org/license/). Its constant tables
(internal/media/av1/tables_gen.go, cdf_gen.go, transform_gen.go) were
extracted by script from the text of that specification and cross-checked
against the read-only data of libaom. The test fixtures were encoded with
libaom, SVT-AV1 and rav1e, and the expected output hashes come from dav1d.

